PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L10U2.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":10,"cefr":"B2+","unit":2,"theme":"Science and Society","essential_question":"Who should decide how far science can go?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1","title":"It May Well Change Medicine: Hedging and Speculation","objectives":["Students will be able to express different degrees of certainty using hedging language.","Students will be able to use appear/seem/tend to and be likely to correctly in academic sentences.","I can write \"The treatment may well change medicine.\"","I can write \"New treatments tend to be expensive at first.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التعبير عن درجات مختلفة من اليقين باستخدام لغة التحفّظ.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام appear/seem/tend to وbe likely to بشكل صحيح في الجمل الأكاديمية.","أستطيع أن أكتب The treatment may well change medicine.","أستطيع أن أكتب New treatments tend to be expensive at first."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"technique","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tɛkˈnik/","definition":"a particular way of doing something that needs skill","arabic":"تقنية / أسلوب","example":"The new technique allows doctors to repair damaged cells.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-W01"},{"word":"precision","pos":"noun","ipa":"/priˈsɪʒən/","definition":"the quality of being very exact and accurate","arabic":"دقة","example":"The robot operates with great precision.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-W02"},{"word":"potential","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈtɛnʃəl/","definition":"the possibility of developing or achieving something in the future","arabic":"إمكانات / قدرة كامنة","example":"The new drug has enormous potential.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-W03"},{"word":"inherited","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈhɛrətɪd/","definition":"passed from parents to children through genes","arabic":"وراثي / موروث","example":"Sickle cell disease is an inherited blood disorder.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"In science and academic writing, careful writers hedge – they show how certain they are instead of presenting every idea as a fact. Strong certainty: will, is bound to, is certain to. Probable: is likely to, will probably, may well. Possible: may, might, could, possibly, perhaps. Unlikely: is unlikely to, probably won't.\nHedging verbs: appear to / seem to (The benefits appear to be enormous.), tend to (for general patterns: New drugs tend to be expensive.), suggest / indicate (The data suggest that…). Impersonal openings: It seems / appears that…, There is a possibility that…, Evidence suggests that….\nHedging adverbs and adjectives: probably, possibly, arguably (it can be argued), relatively (compared with others), approximately (about), largely, generally. Don't over-hedge: one or two hedges in a sentence are enough. (not: It may possibly perhaps be true) → It may be true.\ncertain: will / is bound to • probable: is likely to / may well / probably • possible: may / might / could\nappear / seem / tend + to-infinitive\nIt seems / appears that… • Evidence suggests that…\none or two hedges are enough – don't over-hedge","examples":["The discovery may well change the future of medicine.","The new drug is likely to be approved next year.","Older patients tend to recover more slowly.","The results appear to support the hypothesis.","It seems that the virus spreads mainly through the air.","Evidence suggests that regular exercise reduces the risk of heart disease.","The disease is relatively rare in Europe.","Approximately 300 people are thought to have taken part in the study."],"common_errors":["✗ The new treatment will cure all inherited diseases. → ✓ The new treatment may help to cure some inherited diseases. (Academic writing → hedge strong claims.)","✗ New drugs tend being expensive. → ✓ New drugs tend to be expensive. (tend + to-infinitive.)","✗ The results appear that they are accurate. → ✓ The results appear to be accurate. / It appears that the results are accurate. (Don't mix patterns.)","✗ It may possibly perhaps work. → ✓ It may work. (Don't over-hedge.)"],"arabic":"في الكتابة العلمية يُظهر الكاتب الحريص درجة يقينه بدلًا من تقديم كل فكرة كحقيقة مطلقة (hedging).\nاليقين القوي: will / is bound to. المرجّح: is likely to / may well / probably. الممكن: may / might / could. غير المرجّح: is unlikely to.\nأفعال التحفّظ: appear to / seem to / tend to + المصدر، وIt seems that…، وEvidence suggests that…\nلا نبالغ في التحفّظ: يكفي تحفّظ أو اثنان في الجملة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “It May Well Change Medicine: Hedging and Speculation”. Let’s learn academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U2-LS1 | It May Well Change Medicine: Hedging and Speculation","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “Editing the Code of Life”: In 2012, two scientists described a technique that may well be one of the most important discoveries of the century. The tool, called CRISPR, allows researchers to cut and change DNA with remarkable precision. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"In 2012, two scientists described a technique that may well be one of the most important discoveries of the century. The tool, called CRISPR, allows researchers to cut and change DNA with remarkable precision.","visual":"Image GV-L10-U2-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"In science and academic writing, careful writers hedge – they show how certain they are instead of presenting every idea as a fact. Strong certainty: will, is bound to, is certain to. Probable: is likely to, will probably, may well. Possible: may, might, could, possibly, perhaps. Unlikely: is unlikely to, probably won't. Hedging verbs: appear to / seem to (The benefits appear to be enormous.), tend to (for general patterns: New drugs tend to be expensive.), suggest / indicate (The data suggest that…). Impersonal openings: It seems / appears that…, There is a possibility that…, Evidence suggests that….","on_screen":"certain: will / is bound to • probable: is likely to / may well / probably • possible: may / might / could • appear / seem / tend + to-infinitive • It seems / appears that… • Evidence suggests that…","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The discovery may well change the future of medicine. The new drug is likely to be approved next year. Older patients tend to recover more slowly.","on_screen":"The discovery may well change the future of medicine. / The new drug is likely to be approved next year. / Older patients tend to recover more slowly.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The new treatment will cure all inherited diseases.” That’s not right. We say: “The new treatment may help to cure some inherited diseases.” Academic writing → hedge strong claims.","on_screen":"✗ The new treatment will cure all inherited diseases.   ✓ The new treatment may help to cure some inherited diseases.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Which is more certain: \"may\" or \"is likely to\"? … Think! … *is likely to*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The treatment may well change medicine.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"The treatment may well change medicine.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that….","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","في الكتابة العلمية يُظهر الكاتب الحريص درجة يقينه بدلًا من تقديم كل فكرة كحقيقة مطلقة (hedging).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The new treatment will cure all inherited diseases. — الصحيح: The new treatment may help to cure some inherited diseases.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب The treatment may well change medicine."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Editing the Code of Life","body":"In 2012, two scientists described a technique that may well be one of the most important discoveries of the century. The tool, called CRISPR, allows researchers to cut and change DNA with remarkable precision. In 2020, its inventors were awarded the Nobel Prize in Chemistry.\nThe potential benefits appear to be enormous. In late 2023, the first CRISPR-based treatment was approved for sickle cell disease, an inherited blood disorder that is relatively common in parts of the Arabian Peninsula. Doctors believe that similar therapies are likely to be developed for other inherited diseases within the next decade.\nHowever, gene editing also raises difficult ethical questions. Changes made to human embryos could be passed on to future generations, and scientists cannot yet be certain what the long-term effects might be. Many experts argue that such experiments should be strictly regulated until the risks are better understood.\nCost is another concern. New treatments tend to be extremely expensive, at least at first, which means they are unlikely to be available to everyone. Some researchers suggest that prices will probably fall as the technology improves, but others are less optimistic.\nIt seems that society will have to make important decisions about how far science should go. What is clear is that the conversation cannot be left to scientists alone; doctors, lawmakers, religious scholars and the public will all need to take part.","word_count":229,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"CRISPR ___ one of the most important discoveries of the century.","options":["may be well","well may be","may well be","may well being"],"answer":"may well be","explanation":"Hedging modal (may, might, could, may well).","explanation_ar":"الفعل الناقص للتحفّظ."},{"q":"Similar therapies are likely ___ for other inherited diseases.","options":["to develop by","develop","to be developed","being developed"],"answer":"to be developed","explanation":"be likely / unlikely / bound to.","explanation_ar":"be likely to."},{"q":"New treatments tend ___ extremely expensive at first.","options":["being","to be","that are","be"],"answer":"to be","explanation":"tend to for general patterns.","explanation_ar":"tend to."},{"q":"The potential benefits ___ to be enormous.","options":["appear","appears it","are appearing that","appear that"],"answer":"appear","explanation":"appear / seem + to; It seems / appears that.","explanation_ar":"appear / seem."},{"q":"Which sentence is the most appropriate for a scientific report?","options":["The results prove the treatment cures everything.","The results suggest that the treatment may reduce symptoms.","Everyone knows the treatment works.","The treatment is amazing and works for everyone."],"answer":"The results suggest that the treatment may reduce symptoms.","explanation":"Choose the right degree of certainty.","explanation_ar":"درجة اليقين."},{"q":"Although the early results are encouraging, the researchers caution that the new vaccine ______ effective against every variant of the virus.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["will definitely be","has always been","is certainly","may not be"],"answer":"may not be","explanation":"Choose the right degree of certainty.","explanation_ar":"درجة اليقين."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with the correct hedging form. Word bank: It seems that, is unlikely to, tend to","items":[{"q":"Older patients ______ (tend) recover more slowly after surgery.","options":[],"answer":"tend to","explanation":"tend to for general patterns.","explanation_ar":"tend to."},{"q":"______ (it / seem) the virus spreads mainly through the air.","options":[],"answer":"It seems that","explanation":"appear / seem + to; It seems / appears that.","explanation_ar":"appear / seem."},{"q":"The new medicine ______ (unlikely) be available before 2030.","options":[],"answer":"is unlikely to","explanation":"be likely / unlikely / bound to.","explanation_ar":"be likely to."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Rewrite the claims more carefully, using the hedge in brackets.","items":[{"q":"The new drug will be approved next year. (is likely to)  (hedge)","options":[],"answer":"The new drug is likely to be approved next year.","explanation":"be likely / unlikely / bound to.","explanation_ar":"be likely to."},{"q":"The results are accurate. (appear to)  (hedge)","options":[],"answer":"The results appear to be accurate.","explanation":"appear / seem + to; It seems / appears that.","explanation_ar":"appear / seem."},{"q":"Gene editing will cure inherited diseases. (may well… some)  (hedge)","options":[],"answer":"Gene editing may well cure some inherited diseases.","explanation":"Hedging modal (may, might, could, may well).","explanation_ar":"الفعل الناقص للتحفّظ."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Speculate carefully.","items":[{"q":"Write a hedged prediction about technology in 2040.","options":[],"answer":"By 2040, robots are likely to perform many routine surgeries.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a general pattern with \"tend to\".","options":[],"answer":"Students who sleep well tend to perform better in exams.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence beginning \"Evidence suggests that…\".","options":[],"answer":"Evidence suggests that reading on paper improves memory.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"arguably\".","options":[],"answer":"The internet is arguably the most important invention of the last century.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Rewrite this over-certain claim: \"Social media destroys students' concentration.\"","options":[],"answer":"Social media may reduce some students' ability to concentrate.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A cautious science summary.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 hedged sentences about a new technology (AI, electric cars, gene therapy…).","options":[],"answer":"Artificial intelligence is likely to change many jobs. It may well create new careers. However, its long-term effects appear to be difficult to predict.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences showing two different degrees of certainty about the same topic.","options":[],"answer":"Electric cars will become more common. They may eventually replace petrol cars completely.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence explaining why scientists hedge.","options":[],"answer":"Scientists hedge because new evidence can change what we believe.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spin & Speak","mechanic":"spin_wheel","rules":["Work in teams of four. Player 1 spins the wheel.","Make a correct sentence about the unit theme using the cue word and academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that….","The team checks the sentence with the grammar box. Correct = 1 point; an extra detail = 1 bonus point.","Pass the spinner clockwise. Play for 6 minutes; the team with most points wins."],"data":[{"label":"may well"},{"label":"is likely to"},{"label":"tends to"},{"label":"appears to"},{"label":"It seems that…"},{"label":"must have been"},{"label":"might have caused"},{"label":"may have been affected"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The discovery ___ lead to new treatments for cancer.","options":["well may to","may well","may well to","may be well"],"answer":"may well","explanation":"Hedging modal (may, might, could, may well).","explanation_ar":"الفعل الناقص للتحفّظ."},{"q":"Electric cars are likely ___ more affordable in the coming years.","options":["to becoming","to become","becoming","become"],"answer":"to become","explanation":"be likely / unlikely / bound to.","explanation_ar":"be likely to."},{"q":"Children ___ to learn languages more easily than adults.","options":["are tending that","tends","tend that","tend"],"answer":"tend","explanation":"tend to for general patterns.","explanation_ar":"tend to."},{"q":"","options":["The data appear to support the hypothesis.","The data are appearing that they support the hypothesis.","The data appear supporting the hypothesis.","The data appear that support the hypothesis."],"answer":"The data appear to support the hypothesis.","explanation":"appear / seem + to; It seems / appears that.","explanation_ar":"appear / seem."},{"q":"The disease is ___ rare in Europe but more common in Africa.","options":["relative","relatively to","relatively","relation"],"answer":"relatively","explanation":"Hedging adverb (probably, arguably, relatively, approximately).","explanation_ar":"ظرف التحفّظ."},{"q":"Which sentence shows the lowest degree of certainty?","options":["The treatment is bound to help patients.","The treatment might help some patients.","The treatment will help all patients.","The treatment is certain to help patients."],"answer":"The treatment might help some patients.","explanation":"Choose the right degree of certainty.","explanation_ar":"درجة اليقين."},{"q":"","options":["Approximate 500 volunteers took part in the study.","Approximately of 500 volunteers took part in the study.","Approximation 500 volunteers took part in the study.","Approximately 500 volunteers took part in the study."],"answer":"Approximately 500 volunteers took part in the study.","explanation":"Hedging adverb (probably, arguably, relatively, approximately).","explanation_ar":"ظرف التحفّظ."},{"q":"Because the study included only twenty participants, the researchers concluded that their findings ______ and would need to be confirmed by larger studies.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["proved everything","were conclusive","were certain","were tentative"],"answer":"were tentative","explanation":"Choose the right degree of certainty.","explanation_ar":"درجة اليقين."},{"q":"Studies of people who live to be over one hundred ______ that a combination of diet, activity and strong social connections may contribute to a long life.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["demand","prove","suggest","guarantee"],"answer":"suggest","explanation":"Choose the right degree of certainty.","explanation_ar":"درجة اليقين."},{"q":"\"The results appear to support the theory.\" How certain is the writer?","options":["fairly confident but not completely sure","sure that the theory is wrong","not interested in the results","completely certain"],"answer":"fairly confident but not completely sure","explanation":"appear / seem + to; It seems / appears that.","explanation_ar":"appear / seem."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A modern genetics laboratory: a scientist looking at a DNA double helix on a large screen, a simplified illustration of \"molecular scissors\" cutting a DNA strand, and a panel discussion with a doctor, a lawmaker, a religious scholar and a student at a round table. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A modern genetics laboratory: a scientist looking at a DNA double helix on a large screen, a simplified illustration of \"molecular scissors\" cutting a DNA strand, and a panel discussion with a doctor, a lawmaker, a religious scholar and a student at a round table"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that…, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well)"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows technique (a particular way of doing something that needs skill); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing technique: a particular way of doing something that needs skill"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows precision (the quality of being very exact and accurate); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing precision: the quality of being very exact and accurate"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows potential (the possibility of developing or achieving something in the future); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing potential: the possibility of developing or achieving something in the future"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows inherited (passed from parents to children through genes); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing inherited: passed from parents to children through genes"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spin & Speak”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spin & Speak"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-W01","script":"technique. technique. The new technique allows doctors to repair damaged cells.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-W02","script":"precision. precision. The robot operates with great precision.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-W03","script":"potential. potential. The new drug has enormous potential.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-W04","script":"inherited. inherited. Sickle cell disease is an inherited blood disorder.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-T1","script":"In 2012, two scientists described a technique that may well be one of the most important discoveries of the century. The tool, called CRISPR, allows researchers to cut and change DNA with remarkable precision. In 2020, its inventors were awarded the Nobel Prize in Chemistry. The potential benefits appear to be enormous. In late 2023, the first CRISPR-based treatment was approved for sickle cell disease, an inherited blood disorder that is relatively common in parts of the Arabian Peninsula. Doctors believe that similar therapies are likely to be developed for other inherited diseases within the next decade. However, gene editing also raises difficult ethical questions. Changes made to human embryos could be passed on to future generations, and scientists cannot yet be certain what the long-term effects might be. Many experts argue that such experiments should be strictly regulated until the risks are better understood. Cost is another concern. New treatments tend to be extremely expensive, at least at first, which means they are unlikely to be available to everyone. Some researchers suggest that prices will probably fall as the technology improves, but others are less optimistic. It seems that society will have to make important decisions about how far science should go. What is clear is that the conversation cannot be left to scientists alone; doctors, lawmakers, religious scholars and the public will all need to take part.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “It May Well Change Medicine: Hedging and Speculation”. Let’s learn academic hedging to show degrees of certainty: modal verbs (may, might, could, may well); be likely / unlikely / bound to; appear / seem / tend to; It seems / appears that…; hedging adverbs (probably, possibly, arguably, relatively, approximately); evidence suggests / indicates that… together. Listen to the start of “Editing the Code of Life”: In 2012, two scientists described a technique that may well be one of the most important discoveries of the century. The tool, called CRISPR, allows researchers to cut and change DNA with remarkable precision. Look at the green words. In science and academic writing, careful writers hedge – they show how certain they are instead of presenting every idea as a fact. Strong certainty: will, is bound to, is certain to. Probable: is likely to, will probably, may well. Possible: may, might, could, possibly, perhaps. Unlikely: is unlikely to, probably won't. Hedging verbs: appear to / seem to (The benefits appear to be enormous.), tend to (for general patterns: New drugs tend to be expensive.), suggest / indicate (The data suggest that…). Impersonal openings: It seems / appears that…, There is a possibility that…, Evidence suggests that…. Here are some examples. The discovery may well change the future of medicine. The new drug is likely to be approved next year. Older patients tend to recover more slowly. Be careful! Some learners say: “The new treatment will cure all inherited diseases.” That’s not right. We say: “The new treatment may help to cure some inherited diseases.” Academic writing → hedge strong claims. Quick check: Which is more certain: \"may\" or \"is likely to\"? … Think! … *is likely to*. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The treatment may well change medicine.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2","title":"The Samples May Have Been Contaminated: Modal Perfects for Speculation","objectives":["Students will be able to speculate about past events with modal perfects in active, continuous and passive forms.","Students will be able to choose the modal that matches the degree of certainty.","I can write \"The samples may have been contaminated.\"","I can write \"The scientists must have been working all night.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التخمين عن أحداث ماضية بالأفعال الناقصة التامة في صيغ المعلوم والمستمر والمجهول.","سيتمكن الطلاب من اختيار الفعل الناقص الذي يناسب درجة اليقين.","أستطيع أن أكتب The samples may have been contaminated.","أستطيع أن أكتب The scientists must have been working all night."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"ethical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɛθɪkəl/","definition":"connected with what is morally right or wrong","arabic":"أخلاقي","example":"Gene editing raises serious ethical questions.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS2-W01"},{"word":"controversial","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌkɑntrəˈvɝʃəl/","definition":"causing a lot of disagreement","arabic":"مثير للجدل","example":"The experiment was highly controversial.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS2-W02"},{"word":"long-term","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/lɔŋtɝm/","definition":"lasting for a long time into the future","arabic":"طويل الأمد","example":"Scientists don't yet know the long-term effects.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS2-W03"},{"word":"regulate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈrɛɡjəˌleɪt/","definition":"to control something with rules","arabic":"ينظّم (بقوانين)","example":"Governments regulate how new medicines are tested.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"Use modal + have + past participle to speculate about the past. must have = almost certain: The scientists must have made a mistake. can't have / couldn't have = almost certainly not: They can't have seen the data. may (well) have / might have / could have = possible: The virus might have spread from animals.\nContinuous: modal + have been + -ing for an activity in progress: The team must have been working all night. Passive: modal + have been + past participle: The samples may have been contaminated. The error could have been caused by the heat.\nOther forms: should have + past participle for what was expected: The results should have arrived by now (but they haven't). be likely / unlikely to have + past participle: The fossil is likely to have been buried in a flood. Remember: must have (certain) ≠ had to (obligation in the past).\nmust have done (almost certain) • can't / couldn't have done (almost certainly not)\nmay / might / could have done (possible) • may well have done (quite likely)\ncontinuous: must have been doing • passive: may have been done\nshould have done (expected) • is likely to have done","examples":["The ancient city must have had a sophisticated water system.","The dinosaurs may have been wiped out by an asteroid.","The researchers can't have known about the side effects.","Someone might have been using the equipment without permission.","The results could have been affected by the high temperature.","The data should have been checked before publication.","The early humans are likely to have migrated along the coast.","The error may well have been caused by a faulty sensor."],"common_errors":["✗ The scientists must made a mistake. → ✓ The scientists must have made a mistake. (modal + have + past participle.)","✗ The samples may have contaminated. → ✓ The samples may have been contaminated. (Passive → have been + past participle.)","✗ They mustn't have seen the data. → ✓ They can't have seen the data. (Negative deduction → can't have.)","✗ The team must have been work all night. → ✓ The team must have been working all night. (Continuous → -ing.)"],"arabic":"للتخمين عن الماضي نستخدم الفعل الناقص + have + التصريف الثالث: must have (شبه متأكد)، can't have (شبه متأكد أنه لم يحدث)، may / might / could have (ممكن).\nالمستمر: must have been working، والمجهول: may have been contaminated.\nshould have done لما كان متوقعًا، وis likely to have done للمرجّح.\nانتبه: must have repeated (استنتاج) يختلف عن had to repeat (إلزام في الماضي)."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Samples May Have Been Contaminated: Modal Perfects for Speculation”. Let’s learn speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U2-LS2 | The Samples May Have Been Contaminated: Modal Perfects for Speculation","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: The fire could have been started by a cigarette. She can't have finished the analysis already. Could the results have been affected by the temperature?","on_screen":"The fire could have been started by a cigarette. | She can't have finished the analysis already. | Could the results have been affected by the temperature?","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Use modal + have + past participle to speculate about the past. must have = almost certain: The scientists must have made a mistake. can't have / couldn't have = almost certainly not: They can't have seen the data. may (well) have / might have / could have = possible: The virus might have spread from animals. Continuous: modal + have been + -ing for an activity in progress: The team must have been working all night. Passive: modal + have been + past participle: The samples may have been contaminated. The error could have been caused by the heat.","on_screen":"must have done (almost certain) • can't / couldn't have done (almost certainly not) • may / might / could have done (possible) • may well have done (quite likely) • continuous: must have been doing • passive: may have been done","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The ancient city must have had a sophisticated water system. The dinosaurs may have been wiped out by an asteroid. The researchers can't have known about the side effects.","on_screen":"The ancient city must have had a sophisticated water system. / The dinosaurs may have been wiped out by an asteroid. / The researchers can't have known about the side effects.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The scientists must made a mistake.” That’s not right. We say: “The scientists must have made a mistake.” modal + have + past participle.","on_screen":"✗ The scientists must made a mistake.   ✓ The scientists must have made a mistake.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"The samples may have been contaminated.\" Are we sure? … Think! … No – it's possible. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The samples may have been contaminated.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"The samples may have been contaminated.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","للتخمين عن الماضي نستخدم الفعل الناقص + have + التصريف الثالث: must have (شبه متأكد)، can't have (شبه متأكد أنه لم يحدث)، may / might / could have (ممكن).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The scientists must made a mistake. — الصحيح: The scientists must have made a mistake.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب The samples may have been contaminated."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"The results are completely wrong. The scientists ___ a mistake.","options":["can't have made","must made","must make","must have made"],"answer":"must have made","explanation":"must have + past participle (almost certain).","explanation_ar":"must have."},{"q":"They ___ the data; it wasn't published until today.","options":["must have seen","can't have seen","mustn't have seen","can't see"],"answer":"can't have seen","explanation":"can't / couldn't have (almost certainly not).","explanation_ar":"can't have."},{"q":"The virus ___ from animals to humans, but this hasn't been proven.","options":["must have spread","can't have spread","might have spread","might spread yesterday"],"answer":"might have spread","explanation":"may / might / could have (possible).","explanation_ar":"might have."},{"q":"The lights were on all night. The team ___ late.","options":["must have been work","must have been working","must be working","must have worked late"],"answer":"must have been working","explanation":"Continuous: modal + have been + -ing.","explanation_ar":"المستمر."},{"q":"The unusual results ___ by the high temperature in the lab.","options":["may have caused","may have been cause","may be caused yesterday","may have been caused"],"answer":"may have been caused","explanation":"Passive: modal + have been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"المجهول."},{"q":"Because the fossils were found buried beneath thick layers of mud, paleontologists believe the animals ______ by a sudden flood.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["are likely to have been killed","are likely being killed","likely to have been killed","are likely to have killed"],"answer":"are likely to have been killed","explanation":"should have / is likely to have.","explanation_ar":"should have / likely to have."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with a modal perfect. Word bank: must have had, should have been checked, might have been using","items":[{"q":"The ancient city ______ (must / have) an advanced water system.","options":[],"answer":"must have had","explanation":"must have + past participle (almost certain).","explanation_ar":"must have."},{"q":"Someone ______ (might / use – continuous) the microscope without permission.","options":[],"answer":"might have been using","explanation":"Continuous: modal + have been + -ing.","explanation_ar":"المستمر."},{"q":"The data ______ (should / check – passive) before publication.","options":[],"answer":"should have been checked","explanation":"should have / is likely to have.","explanation_ar":"should have / likely to have."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"The dinosaurs may have wiped out by an asteroid.","options":[],"answer":"The dinosaurs may have been wiped out by an asteroid.","explanation":"may have wiped → may have been wiped. Passive: modal + have been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: may have wiped → may have been wiped. المجهول."},{"q":"The researchers mustn't have known about the side effects.","options":[],"answer":"The researchers can't have known about the side effects.","explanation":"mustn't have known → can't have known. can't / couldn't have (almost certainly not).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: mustn't have known → can't have known. can't have."},{"q":"The scientists must made an error in their calculations.","options":[],"answer":"The scientists must have made an error in their calculations.","explanation":"must made → must have made. must have + past participle (almost certain).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: must made → must have made. must have."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Speculate about the past.","items":[{"q":"The experiment failed. Write two possible explanations.","options":[],"answer":"The equipment might have been faulty. The samples may have been contaminated.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"must have\" about an ancient civilization.","options":[],"answer":"The Nabataeans must have been skilled engineers.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"can't have\" about a mistake.","options":[],"answer":"The student can't have read the instructions carefully.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"should have\" about something that didn't happen.","options":[],"answer":"The results should have arrived yesterday.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a passive modal perfect about a mystery.","options":[],"answer":"The ancient tablets may have been hidden by priests.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A lab investigation.","items":[{"q":"Describe an unexpected result in an experiment (1 sentence).","options":[],"answer":"The plants in our experiment died after three days.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 3 speculations with different degrees of certainty.","options":[],"answer":"They must have been given too much water. They might have been damaged by the cold. They can't have lacked sunlight because they were near the window.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence about what should have been done.","options":[],"answer":"We should have measured the water more carefully.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Tic-Tac-Grammar","mechanic":"tic_tac_toe","rules":["Two teams: X and O. Team X chooses a square.","Using the cue, the team says a correct sentence with speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle (the form in brackets: +, – or ?).","If the class and teacher agree it is correct, the team marks the square.","Three squares in a row wins. Play a second round with the grid turned around."],"data":[{"cue":"the treatment / may well / change medicine"},{"cue":"prices / be likely / fall"},{"cue":"new drugs / tend / be expensive"},{"cue":"the results / appear / be accurate"},{"cue":"it / seem / that / the vaccine / work"},{"cue":"the scientists / must / make / a mistake (past)"},{"cue":"the virus / might / spread / from animals (past)"},{"cue":"the samples / may / contaminate (past passive)"},{"cue":"the researchers / can't / see / the data (past)"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The fossil is millions of years old. It ___ in a very dry place.","options":["must preserve","must be preserved yesterday","must have been preserved","must have preserved"],"answer":"must have been preserved","explanation":"must have + past participle (almost certain).","explanation_ar":"must have."},{"q":"She ___ the experiment already; she only started an hour ago.","options":["might finish","can't have finished","can't finish yesterday","must have finished"],"answer":"can't have finished","explanation":"can't / couldn't have (almost certainly not).","explanation_ar":"can't have."},{"q":"The patient's symptoms ___ by an allergy, but the doctors aren't sure.","options":["might caused","might have caused","might be cause","might have been caused"],"answer":"might have been caused","explanation":"Passive: modal + have been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"المجهول."},{"q":"The tired researchers ___ for days without proper sleep.","options":["must have been working","must be worked","must have been worked","must have work"],"answer":"must have been working","explanation":"Continuous: modal + have been + -ing.","explanation_ar":"المستمر."},{"q":"","options":["The asteroid may well have changed Earth's climate.","The asteroid may well changed Earth's climate.","The asteroid may have well changed Earth's climate.","The asteroid well may have change Earth's climate."],"answer":"The asteroid may well have changed Earth's climate.","explanation":"may / might / could have (possible).","explanation_ar":"might have."},{"q":"","options":["The report should have published last month.","The report should be published last month.","The report should had been published last month.","The report should have been published last month."],"answer":"The report should have been published last month.","explanation":"should have / is likely to have.","explanation_ar":"should have / likely to have."},{"q":"\"The scientists must have repeated the test.\" Is this a fact or a deduction?","options":["a deduction","a future plan","an obligation","a fact we know for sure"],"answer":"a deduction","explanation":"must have + past participle (almost certain).","explanation_ar":"must have."},{"q":"The ancient settlement near Tayma ______ for thousands of years, since archaeologists have found tools from many different periods there.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["must have occupied","must have been occupied","must be occupied","must had been occupied"],"answer":"must have been occupied","explanation":"Passive: modal + have been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"المجهول."},{"q":"Because the patient showed no improvement after the treatment, the doctors concluded that the dose ______ too low.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["might be yesterday","might been","might have been","might have be"],"answer":"might have been","explanation":"may / might / could have (possible).","explanation_ar":"might have."},{"q":"Which sentence expresses past obligation, not speculation?","options":["The researchers might have repeated the test.","The researchers can't have repeated the test.","The researchers had to repeat the test.","The researchers must have repeated the test."],"answer":"The researchers had to repeat the test.","explanation":"must have + past participle (almost certain).","explanation_ar":"must have."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A laboratory investigation: an unexpected result on a screen marked \"Error?\", a broken seal on a sample tube, a clock showing 3:00 a.m. and tired scientists with coffee cups; a detective-style notebook with possible explanations. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A laboratory investigation: an unexpected result on a screen marked \"Error?\", a broken seal on a sample tube, a clock showing 3:00 a.m. and tired scientists with coffee cups; a detective-style notebook with possible explanations"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle: a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Science and Society”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows ethical (connected with what is morally right or wrong); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing ethical: connected with what is morally right or wrong"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows controversial (causing a lot of disagreement); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing controversial: causing a lot of disagreement"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows long-term (lasting for a long time into the future); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing long-term: lasting for a long time into the future"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows regulate (to control something with rules); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing regulate: to control something with rules"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Tic-Tac-Grammar”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Tic-Tac-Grammar"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS2-W01","script":"ethical. ethical. Gene editing raises serious ethical questions.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS2-W02","script":"controversial. controversial. The experiment was highly controversial.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS2-W03","script":"long-term. long-term. Scientists don't yet know the long-term effects.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS2-W04","script":"regulate. regulate. Governments regulate how new medicines are tested.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Samples May Have Been Contaminated: Modal Perfects for Speculation”. Let’s learn speculating about the past: must have / can't have / couldn't have / may (well) have / might have / could have + past participle; continuous (might have been working) and passive (may have been contaminated) forms; should have (expectation); be likely to have + past participle together. Remember these patterns: The fire could have been started by a cigarette. She can't have finished the analysis already. Could the results have been affected by the temperature? Use modal + have + past participle to speculate about the past. must have = almost certain: The scientists must have made a mistake. can't have / couldn't have = almost certainly not: They can't have seen the data. may (well) have / might have / could have = possible: The virus might have spread from animals. Continuous: modal + have been + -ing for an activity in progress: The team must have been working all night. Passive: modal + have been + past participle: The samples may have been contaminated. The error could have been caused by the heat. Here are some examples. The ancient city must have had a sophisticated water system. The dinosaurs may have been wiped out by an asteroid. The researchers can't have known about the side effects. Be careful! Some learners say: “The scientists must made a mistake.” That’s not right. We say: “The scientists must have made a mistake.” modal + have + past participle. Quick check: \"The samples may have been contaminated.\" Are we sure? … Think! … No – it's possible. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The samples may have been contaminated.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3","title":"Tentative, Plausible, Conclusive: The Vocabulary of Scientific Debate","objectives":["Students will be able to use precise vocabulary to discuss evidence and ethics in science.","Students will be able to distinguish confusable academic words and form irregular academic plurals.","I can explain the difference between \"tentative\" and \"conclusive\" results.","I can use \"imply\" and \"infer\" correctly."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام مفردات دقيقة لمناقشة الأدلة والأخلاقيات في العلم.","سيتمكن الطلاب من التمييز بين الكلمات الأكاديمية المتشابهة وتكوين الجموع الأكاديمية الشاذة.","أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين النتائج المبدئية والقاطعة.","أستطيع أن أستخدم imply وinfer بشكل صحيح."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"implication","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪmpləˈkeɪʃən/","definition":"a possible effect or result of an action or decision","arabic":"تبعة / انعكاس","example":"The discovery has important implications for medicine.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W01"},{"word":"plausible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈplɔzəbəl/","definition":"seeming reasonable and possibly true","arabic":"معقول / مقنع","example":"The scientist offered a plausible explanation.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W02"},{"word":"conclusive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/kənˈklusɪv/","definition":"proving something completely","arabic":"قاطع / حاسم","example":"There is no conclusive evidence yet.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W03"},{"word":"tentative","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈtɛntətɪv/","definition":"not certain or final","arabic":"مبدئي / غير نهائي","example":"The results are tentative and need more testing.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W04"},{"word":"speculate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈspɛkjəˌleɪt/","definition":"to guess about something without enough information","arabic":"يخمّن / يتكهّن","example":"Journalists speculated about the cause of the illness.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W05"},{"word":"consent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈsɛnt/","definition":"permission to do something","arabic":"موافقة","example":"Patients must give their consent before an operation.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W06"},{"word":"unprecedented","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ənˈprɛsɪˌdɛntɪd/","definition":"never having happened before","arabic":"غير مسبوق","example":"The pandemic caused unprecedented changes in daily life.","image_id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"Evidence words: conclusive evidence proves something completely; inconclusive evidence doesn't; tentative results are early and not final; a plausible explanation seems reasonable; to speculate is to guess without enough information; unprecedented means never seen before. Ethics words: ethical, controversial, consent (permission), regulate (control with rules), implication (a possible effect).\nHedging verbs: suggest (The data suggest…), indicate (The results indicate…), imply (the speaker or writer hints something). infer = the reader or listener reaches a conclusion: The report implies that the drug is unsafe; readers infer that it should be withdrawn. Other confusables: affect (verb: influence) vs effect (noun: result); precise (exact, detailed) vs accurate (correct).\nSome academic words keep Greek or Latin plurals: phenomenon → phenomena, criterion → criteria, hypothesis → hypotheses, analysis → analyses, thesis → theses, datum → data. Use them carefully: (not: a criteria) → a criterion; these phenomena are…\nCollocations/phrases: conclusive evidence • inconclusive results • tentative findings • a plausible explanation | speculate about the cause • give informed consent • raise ethical questions • have implications for | imply (writer) • infer (reader) • affect (verb) • effect (noun) | phenomenon → phenomena • criterion → criteria • hypothesis → hypotheses • analysis → analyses","examples":["There is no conclusive evidence that the supplement works.","The researchers offered a plausible explanation for the strange results.","The findings are tentative and need further testing.","Patients must give informed consent before joining a trial.","The discovery has major implications for the treatment of cancer.","The article implies that the company ignored the risks.","Lack of sleep can affect memory; its effects can be serious.","The scientists tested three hypotheses."],"common_errors":["✗ The evidence is conclusive, so we need more research to be sure. → ✓ The evidence is inconclusive, so we need more research. (Not proven → inconclusive.)","✗ From the report, I implied that the drug was unsafe. → ✓ From the report, I inferred that the drug was unsafe. (The reader infers.)","✗ The new rule will effect thousands of patients. → ✓ The new rule will affect thousands of patients. (Verb → affect.)","✗ This criteria is very strict. → ✓ This criterion is very strict. / These criteria are very strict. (Singular criterion, plural criteria.)"],"arabic":"كلمات الأدلة: conclusive (قاطع) وinconclusive (غير قاطع) وtentative (مبدئي) وplausible (معقول) وspeculate (يتكهّن) وunprecedented (غير مسبوق).\nكلمات الأخلاقيات: ethical (أخلاقي) وcontroversial (مثير للجدل) وconsent (موافقة) وregulate (ينظّم) وimplication (تبعة).\nimply للكاتب الذي يلمّح، وinfer للقارئ الذي يستنتج. affect فعل وeffect اسم.\nجموع أكاديمية: phenomenon → phenomena، criterion → criteria، hypothesis → hypotheses."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Tentative, Plausible, Conclusive: The Vocabulary of Scientific Debate”. Let’s learn academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U2-LS3 | Tentative, Plausible, Conclusive: The Vocabulary of Scientific Debate","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. implication: a possible effect or result of an action or decision. plausible: seeming reasonable and possibly true. conclusive: proving something completely.","on_screen":"implication | plausible | conclusive","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Evidence words: conclusive evidence proves something completely; inconclusive evidence doesn't; tentative results are early and not final; a plausible explanation seems reasonable; to speculate is to guess without enough information; unprecedented means never seen before. Ethics words: ethical, controversial, consent (permission), regulate (control with rules), implication (a possible effect). Hedging verbs: suggest (The data suggest…), indicate (The results indicate…), imply (the speaker or writer hints something). infer = the reader or listener reaches a conclusion: The report implies that the drug is unsafe; readers infer that it should be withdrawn. Other confusables: affect (verb: influence) vs effect (noun: result); precise (exact, detailed) vs accurate (correct).","on_screen":"academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. There is no conclusive evidence that the supplement works. The researchers offered a plausible explanation for the strange results. The findings are tentative and need further testing.","on_screen":"There is no conclusive evidence that the supplement works. / The researchers offered a plausible explanation for the strange results. / The findings are tentative and need further testing.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The evidence is conclusive, so we need more research to be sure.” That’s not right. We say: “The evidence is inconclusive, so we need more research.” Not proven → inconclusive.","on_screen":"✗ The evidence is conclusive, so we need more research to be sure.   ✓ The evidence is inconclusive, so we need more research.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: If results are \"tentative,\" are they final? … Think! … No. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the difference between \"tentative\" and \"conclusive\" results.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can explain the difference between \"tentative\" and \"conclusive\" results.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","كلمات الأدلة: conclusive (قاطع) وinconclusive (غير قاطع) وtentative (مبدئي) وplausible (معقول) وspeculate (يتكهّن) وunprecedented (غير مسبوق).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The evidence is conclusive, so we need more research to be sure. — الصحيح: The evidence is inconclusive, so we need more research.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أشرح الفرق بين النتائج المبدئية والقاطعة."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"There is no ___ evidence that the supplement improves memory.","options":["tentative","plausible","conclusive","ethical"],"answer":"conclusive","explanation":"Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The results are only ___; the team needs to test more patients.","options":["controversial","unprecedented","tentative","conclusive"],"answer":"tentative","explanation":"Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"From the scientist's tone, the audience ___ that she disagreed with the decision.","options":["effected","affected","implied","inferred"],"answer":"inferred","explanation":"Confusable pair (imply/infer, affect/effect, precise/accurate).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة."},{"q":"Lack of sleep can seriously ___ your concentration.","options":["affect","infer","effect","imply"],"answer":"affect","explanation":"Confusable pair (imply/infer, affect/effect, precise/accurate).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة."},{"q":"The researchers tested three different ___.","options":["hypothesises","hypotheses","hypothesi","hypothesis"],"answer":"hypotheses","explanation":"Irregular academic plural.","explanation_ar":"الجمع الشاذ."},{"q":"Before joining the clinical trial, every volunteer was required to give written ______, confirming that they understood the possible risks.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["implication","consent","speculation","precision"],"answer":"consent","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"plausible","options":["causing a lot of disagreement","to control with rules","a possible effect or result","to guess without enough information","seeming reasonable and possibly true","never having happened before"],"answer":"seeming reasonable and possibly true","explanation":"plausible → seeming reasonable and possibly true. Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"speculate","options":["causing a lot of disagreement","to control with rules","a possible effect or result","to guess without enough information","seeming reasonable and possibly true","never having happened before"],"answer":"to guess without enough information","explanation":"speculate → to guess without enough information. Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"unprecedented","options":["causing a lot of disagreement","to control with rules","a possible effect or result","to guess without enough information","seeming reasonable and possibly true","never having happened before"],"answer":"never having happened before","explanation":"unprecedented → never having happened before. Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"implication","options":["causing a lot of disagreement","to control with rules","a possible effect or result","to guess without enough information","seeming reasonable and possibly true","never having happened before"],"answer":"a possible effect or result","explanation":"implication → a possible effect or result. Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"regulate","options":["causing a lot of disagreement","to control with rules","a possible effect or result","to guess without enough information","seeming reasonable and possibly true","never having happened before"],"answer":"to control with rules","explanation":"regulate → to control with rules. Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"controversial","options":["causing a lot of disagreement","to control with rules","a possible effect or result","to guess without enough information","seeming reasonable and possibly true","never having happened before"],"answer":"causing a lot of disagreement","explanation":"controversial → causing a lot of disagreement. Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Use precise vocabulary.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with \"plausible\".","options":[],"answer":"Climate change is a plausible explanation for the shrinking glaciers.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence using \"imply\" and one using \"infer\".","options":[],"answer":"The coach's silence implied that he was disappointed. We inferred that we had played badly.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"unprecedented\".","options":[],"answer":"The heat wave reached unprecedented temperatures.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"criteria\".","options":[],"answer":"The university has strict criteria for scholarships.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Name a controversial scientific topic and explain why it is controversial.","options":[],"answer":"Artificial intelligence is controversial because it may replace some jobs.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Science debate notes.","items":[{"q":"Choose a scientific issue and write 2 sentences about its implications.","options":[],"answer":"Self-driving cars have implications for road safety. They may also affect millions of drivers' jobs.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences about the evidence: one tentative, one conclusive.","options":[],"answer":"The evidence that coffee improves memory is tentative. The evidence that smoking causes cancer is conclusive.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence about an ethical question science must answer.","options":[],"answer":"Should scientists be allowed to edit human genes before birth?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Memory Match","mechanic":"matching","rules":["Place all cards face down in a grid.","Turn over two cards. If the word matches its definition, keep the pair and say a sentence with the word.","If not, turn them back over; the next player plays.","The player with the most pairs wins."],"data":[{"word":"technique","match":"a particular way of doing something that needs skill","arabic":"تقنية / أسلوب"},{"word":"precision","match":"the quality of being very exact and accurate","arabic":"دقة"},{"word":"potential","match":"the possibility of developing or achieving something in the future","arabic":"إمكانات / قدرة كامنة"},{"word":"inherited","match":"passed from parents to children through genes","arabic":"وراثي / موروث"},{"word":"ethical","match":"connected with what is morally right or wrong","arabic":"أخلاقي"},{"word":"controversial","match":"causing a lot of disagreement","arabic":"مثير للجدل"},{"word":"long-term","match":"lasting for a long time into the future","arabic":"طويل الأمد"},{"word":"regulate","match":"to control something with rules","arabic":"ينظّم (بقوانين)"},{"word":"implication","match":"a possible effect or result of an action or decision","arabic":"تبعة / انعكاس"},{"word":"plausible","match":"seeming reasonable and possibly true","arabic":"معقول / مقنع"},{"word":"conclusive","match":"proving something completely","arabic":"قاطع / حاسم"},{"word":"tentative","match":"not certain or final","arabic":"مبدئي / غير نهائي"},{"word":"speculate","match":"to guess about something without enough information","arabic":"يخمّن / يتكهّن"},{"word":"consent","match":"permission to do something","arabic":"موافقة"},{"word":"unprecedented","match":"never having happened before","arabic":"غير مسبوق"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The scientist offered a ___ explanation that most experts accepted.","options":["unprecedented","plausible","tentative","conclusive"],"answer":"plausible","explanation":"Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Journalists began to ___ about the cause of the illness before the doctors had finished their tests.","options":["infer","regulate","speculate","consent"],"answer":"speculate","explanation":"Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The pandemic caused ___ changes in the way people work.","options":["tentative","inherited","plausible","unprecedented"],"answer":"unprecedented","explanation":"Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The new law will ___ how companies use personal data.","options":["infer","effect","speculate","regulate"],"answer":"regulate","explanation":"Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The ___ of the new medicine were clear after only two weeks.","options":["effects","affects","implies","infers"],"answer":"effects","explanation":"Confusable pair (imply/infer, affect/effect, precise/accurate).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة."},{"q":"These strange ___ have never been explained.","options":["phenomenas","phenomenon","phenomena","phenomenons"],"answer":"phenomena","explanation":"Irregular academic plural.","explanation_ar":"الجمع الشاذ."},{"q":"The thermometer gives very ___ measurements, to one-hundredth of a degree.","options":["implied","precise","tentative","accurate guess"],"answer":"precise","explanation":"Confusable pair (imply/infer, affect/effect, precise/accurate).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة."},{"q":"Which word describes evidence that proves something completely?","options":["speculative","conclusive","tentative","inconclusive"],"answer":"conclusive","explanation":"Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Because the experiment raised serious questions about animal welfare, many scientists considered it ______ and called for stricter rules.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["inherited","conclusive","unethical","precise"],"answer":"unethical","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The author never states directly that the policy failed, but the statistics she presents strongly ______ that it did.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["imply","affect","infer","regulate"],"answer":"imply","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"A science debate in a university lecture hall: a screen showing a graph labelled \"Tentative results\", students holding cards with \"Conclusive?\", \"Ethical?\", \"Implications?\"; a moderator with a microphone. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A science debate in a university lecture hall: a screen showing a graph labelled \"Tentative results\", students holding cards with \"Conclusive?\", \"Ethical?\", \"Implications?\"; a moderator with a microphone"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows implication (a possible effect or result of an action or decision); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing implication: a possible effect or result of an action or decision"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows plausible (seeming reasonable and possibly true); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing plausible: seeming reasonable and possibly true"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows conclusive (proving something completely); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing conclusive: proving something completely"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows tentative (not certain or final); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing tentative: not certain or final"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows speculate (to guess about something without enough information); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing speculate: to guess about something without enough information"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows consent (permission to do something); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing consent: permission to do something"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows unprecedented (never having happened before); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing unprecedented: never having happened before"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Memory Match”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Memory Match"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W01","script":"implication. implication. The discovery has important implications for medicine.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W02","script":"plausible. plausible. The scientist offered a plausible explanation.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W03","script":"conclusive. conclusive. There is no conclusive evidence yet.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W04","script":"tentative. tentative. The results are tentative and need more testing.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W05","script":"speculate. speculate. Journalists speculated about the cause of the illness.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W06","script":"consent. consent. Patients must give their consent before an operation.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-W07","script":"unprecedented. unprecedented. The pandemic caused unprecedented changes in daily life.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Tentative, Plausible, Conclusive: The Vocabulary of Scientific Debate”. Let’s learn academic and ethical vocabulary for scientific debate (implication, plausible, conclusive / inconclusive, tentative, speculate, consent, unprecedented, controversial, regulate); hedging verbs (suggest, indicate, imply); confusable pairs (imply / infer; affect / effect; precise / accurate); irregular academic plurals (phenomenon / phenomena; criterion / criteria; hypothesis / hypotheses) together. Meet some new words. implication: a possible effect or result of an action or decision. plausible: seeming reasonable and possibly true. conclusive: proving something completely. Evidence words: conclusive evidence proves something completely; inconclusive evidence doesn't; tentative results are early and not final; a plausible explanation seems reasonable; to speculate is to guess without enough information; unprecedented means never seen before. Ethics words: ethical, controversial, consent (permission), regulate (control with rules), implication (a possible effect). Hedging verbs: suggest (The data suggest…), indicate (The results indicate…), imply (the speaker or writer hints something). infer = the reader or listener reaches a conclusion: The report implies that the drug is unsafe; readers infer that it should be withdrawn. Other confusables: affect (verb: influence) vs effect (noun: result); precise (exact, detailed) vs accurate (correct). Here are some examples. There is no conclusive evidence that the supplement works. The researchers offered a plausible explanation for the strange results. The findings are tentative and need further testing. Be careful! Some learners say: “The evidence is conclusive, so we need more research to be sure.” That’s not right. We say: “The evidence is inconclusive, so we need more research.” Not proven → inconclusive. Quick check: If results are \"tentative,\" are they final? … Think! … No. Great job! Now you can say: I can explain the difference between \"tentative\" and \"conclusive\" results.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS4","title":"The Scientists' Results, Its Effects: Possessives and Apostrophes (SAT)","objectives":["Students will be able to form singular, plural and irregular possessives correctly.","Students will be able to distinguish possessives from plurals and contractions in SAT-style questions.","I can write \"the scientists' results\" and \"the children's hospital\".","I can choose between its and it's, and between scientists and scientists'."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من تكوين الملكية للمفرد والجمع والجمع الشاذ بشكل صحيح.","سيتمكن الطلاب من التمييز بين الملكية والجمع والاختصار في أسئلة بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أكتب the scientists' results وthe children's hospital.","أستطيع أن أختار بين its وit's، وبين scientists وscientists'."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"technique","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tɛkˈnik/","definition":"a particular way of doing something that needs skill","arabic":"تقنية / أسلوب","example":"The new technique allows doctors to repair damaged cells.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"precision","pos":"noun","ipa":"/priˈsɪʒən/","definition":"the quality of being very exact and accurate","arabic":"دقة","example":"The robot operates with great precision.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"potential","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈtɛnʃəl/","definition":"the possibility of developing or achieving something in the future","arabic":"إمكانات / قدرة كامنة","example":"The new drug has enormous potential.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"inherited","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈhɛrətɪd/","definition":"passed from parents to children through genes","arabic":"وراثي / موروث","example":"Sickle cell disease is an inherited blood disorder.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"ethical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɛθɪkəl/","definition":"connected with what is morally right or wrong","arabic":"أخلاقي","example":"Gene editing raises serious ethical questions.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"controversial","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌkɑntrəˈvɝʃəl/","definition":"causing a lot of disagreement","arabic":"مثير للجدل","example":"The experiment was highly controversial.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Singular noun → add 's: the scientist's notebook, Lina's project, James's lab (or James' – both are used; be consistent). Plural noun ending in -s → add only ': the scientists' results (more than one scientist). Irregular plural (not ending in -s) → add 's: the children's hospital, the people's opinions, women's health.\nJoint possession (one thing shared) → apostrophe on the last name: Saad and Lina's project (one project). Separate possession → apostrophe on each name: Saad's and Lina's projects (two projects). Don't use apostrophes for ordinary plurals ((not: the scientist's agreed) → the scientists agreed) or decades (the 1990s).\nPossessive pronouns have no apostrophe: its, their, whose, yours, ours. Contractions do: it's (it is / it has), they're (they are), who's (who is). (not: The company changed it's name) → its name. SAT questions often give four options such as scientists, scientist's, scientists' and scientist's' – decide: one or more owners? possession or just plural?\nsingular + 's • plural -s + ' • irregular plural + 's\njoint: A and B's • separate: A's and B's\nits / their / whose (possessive – no apostrophe) • it's / they're / who's (contractions)\nno apostrophe for simple plurals or decades (1990s)","examples":["The scientist's notebook was found in the lab. (one scientist)","The scientists' results were published in a journal. (several scientists)","The children's ward has new equipment.","Saad and Lina's project won first prize. (one shared project)","The company improved its safety procedures.","It's likely that the treatment will be approved.","The researchers presented their findings; they're very proud of them.","In the 1990s, few people had mobile phones."],"common_errors":["✗ The scientist's agreed on the results. → ✓ The scientists agreed on the results. (Simple plural → no apostrophe.)","✗ The company changed it's name. → ✓ The company changed its name. (Possessive its – no apostrophe.)","✗ The childrens' hospital opened in 2020. → ✓ The children's hospital opened in 2020. (Irregular plural + 's.)","✗ The two researchers's findings were similar. → ✓ The two researchers' findings were similar. (Plural ending in -s → add only '.)"],"arabic":"المفرد + 's: the scientist's notebook، والجمع المنتهي بـ s + ': the scientists' results، والجمع الشاذ + 's: the children's hospital.\nالملكية المشتركة: Saad and Lina's project، والملكية المنفصلة: Saad's and Lina's projects.\nالضمائر الملكية بلا فاصلة عليا: its وtheir وwhose، أما الاختصارات ففيها: it's وthey're وwho's.\nلا فاصلة عليا للجمع العادي أو للعقود (1990s). وأسئلة SAT تختبر هذا كثيرًا."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Scientists' Results, Its Effects: Possessives and Apostrophes (SAT)”. Let’s learn possessive apostrophes: singular ('s), plural (s'), irregular plural (children's), names ending in -s (James's); joint vs separate possession; its vs it's, their / there / they're, whose / who's; plural vs possessive (scientists / scientist's / scientists'); no apostrophe for plurals or decades (the 1990s) together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U2-LS4 | The Scientists' Results, Its Effects: Possessives and Apostrophes (SAT)","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Singular noun → add 's: the scientist's notebook, Lina's project, James's lab (or James' – both are used; be consistent). Plural noun ending in -s → add only ': the scientists' results (more than one scientist). Irregular plural (not ending in -s) → add 's: the children's hospital, the people's opinions, women's health.","on_screen":"possessive apostrophes: singular ('s), plural (s'), irregular plural (children's), names ending in -s (James's); joint vs separate possession; its vs it's, their / there / they're, whose / who's; plural vs possessive (scientists / scientist's / scientists'); no apostrophe for plurals or decades (the 1990s)","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Singular noun → add 's: the scientist's notebook, Lina's project, James's lab (or James' – both are used; be consistent). Plural noun ending in -s → add only ': the scientists' results (more than one scientist). Irregular plural (not ending in -s) → add 's: the children's hospital, the people's opinions, women's health. Joint possession (one thing shared) → apostrophe on the last name: Saad and Lina's project (one project). Separate possession → apostrophe on each name: Saad's and Lina's projects (two projects). Don't use apostrophes for ordinary plurals ((not: the scientist's agreed) → the scientists agreed) or decades (the 1990s).","on_screen":"singular + 's • plural -s + ' • irregular plural + 's • joint: A and B's • separate: A's and B's • its / their / whose (possessive – no apostrophe) • it's / they're / who's (contractions)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The scientist's notebook was found in the lab. (one scientist) The scientists' results were published in a journal. (several scientists) The children's ward has new equipment.","on_screen":"The scientist's notebook was found in the lab. (one scientist) / The scientists' results were published in a journal. (several scientists) / The children's ward has new equipment.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The scientist's agreed on the results.” That’s not right. We say: “The scientists agreed on the results.” Simple plural → no apostrophe.","on_screen":"✗ The scientist's agreed on the results.   ✓ The scientists agreed on the results.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"The scientists' results\" – one scientist or more? … Think! … More than one. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"the scientists' results\" and \"the children's hospital\".","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"the scientists' results\" and \"the children's hospital\".","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: possessive apostrophes: singular ('s), plural (s'), irregular plural (children's), names ending in -s (James's); joint vs separate possession; its vs it's, their / there / they're, whose / who's; plural vs possessive (scientists / scientist's / scientists'); no apostrophe for plurals or decades (the 1990s).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","المفرد + 's: the scientist's notebook، والجمع المنتهي بـ s + ': the scientists' results، والجمع الشاذ + 's: the children's hospital.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The scientist's agreed on the results. — الصحيح: The scientists agreed on the results.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب the scientists' results وthe children's hospital."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Lab News (editing passage)","body":"The researcher's new study, which involved five teams, was published yesterday. It's main finding is that the treatment helps childrens' lungs recover faster. Dr. Saad and Dr. Huda's separate studies reached similar conclusions. In the 1990's, such research would have been impossible.","word_count":42,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"The two ___ findings were published in the same journal.","options":["researcher's","researchers","researchers's","researchers'"],"answer":"researchers'","explanation":"Plural possessive (s').","explanation_ar":"ملكية الجمع."},{"q":"The new ___ ward has the latest equipment.","options":["childrens","children's","children'","childrens'"],"answer":"children's","explanation":"Irregular plural possessive (children's).","explanation_ar":"الجمع الشاذ."},{"q":"The company improved ___ safety procedures after the accident.","options":["its","it's","its'","it is"],"answer":"its","explanation":"its / it's, their / they're, whose / who's.","explanation_ar":"its أم it's."},{"q":"___ project won first prize; they worked on it together.","options":["Saad's and Lina's","Saad and Linas","Saad and Lina's","Saads and Lina's"],"answer":"Saad and Lina's","explanation":"Joint or separate possession.","explanation_ar":"الملكية المشتركة أو المنفصلة."},{"q":"","options":["The scientists' agreed on the results.","The scientist's agreed on the results.","The scientists's agreed on the results.","The scientists agreed on the results."],"answer":"The scientists agreed on the results.","explanation":"Plural or possessive? (no apostrophe for plurals).","explanation_ar":"جمع أم ملكية؟"},{"q":"After years of research, the ______ discovery was finally recognized with an international award for chemistry.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["team's","team","teams","teams'"],"answer":"team's","explanation":"Singular possessive ('s).","explanation_ar":"ملكية المفرد."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS4-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Write the correct form. Word bank: doctors', people's, It's","items":[{"q":"Both ______ (doctors) opinions were included in the report.","options":[],"answer":"doctors'","explanation":"Plural possessive (s').","explanation_ar":"ملكية الجمع."},{"q":"______ (it is) likely that the new vaccine will be approved.","options":[],"answer":"It's","explanation":"its / it's, their / they're, whose / who's.","explanation_ar":"its أم it's."},{"q":"Many ______ (people) views on gene editing have changed.","options":[],"answer":"people's","explanation":"Irregular plural possessive (children's).","explanation_ar":"الجمع الشاذ."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"The university announced it's new research center.","options":[],"answer":"The university announced its new research center.","explanation":"it's → its. its / it's, their / they're, whose / who's.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: it's → its. its أم it's."},{"q":"The scientist's in the lab worked through the night.","options":[],"answer":"The scientists in the lab worked through the night.","explanation":"scientist's → scientists. Plural or possessive? (no apostrophe for plurals).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: scientist's → scientists. جمع أم ملكية؟"},{"q":"Womens' health is the focus of the new study.","options":[],"answer":"Women's health is the focus of the new study.","explanation":"Womens' → Women's. Irregular plural possessive (children's).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Womens' → Women's. الجمع الشاذ."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the science news.","items":[{"q":"The researcher's new study, which involved five teams, was published yesterday. Its main finding is that the treatment helps children's lungs recover faster. [Dr.","options":[],"answer":"The researchers' new study, which involved five teams, was published yesterday. Its main finding is that the treatment helps children's lungs recover faster. [Dr.","explanation":"researcher's → researchers'. Singular possessive ('s).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: researcher's → researchers'. ملكية المفرد."},{"q":"The researchers' new study, which involved five teams, was published yesterday. It's main finding is that the treatment helps children's lungs recover faster. [Dr.","options":[],"answer":"The researchers' new study, which involved five teams, was published yesterday. Its main finding is that the treatment helps children's lungs recover faster. [Dr.","explanation":"It's → Its. Singular possessive ('s).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: It's → Its. ملكية المفرد."},{"q":"The researchers' new study, which involved five teams, was published yesterday. Its main finding is that the treatment helps childrens' lungs recover faster. [Dr.","options":[],"answer":"The researchers' new study, which involved five teams, was published yesterday. Its main finding is that the treatment helps children's lungs recover faster. [Dr.","explanation":"childrens' → children's. Singular possessive ('s).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: childrens' → children's. ملكية المفرد."},{"q":"Dr. Saad and Dr. Huda's","options":[],"answer":"Dr. Saad's and Dr. Huda's","explanation":"Dr. Saad and Dr. Huda's → Dr. Saad's and Dr. Huda's. Singular possessive ('s).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Dr. Saad and Dr. Huda's → Dr. Saad's and Dr. Huda's. ملكية المفرد."},{"q":"In the 1990's, such research would have been impossible.","options":[],"answer":"In the 1990s, such research would have been impossible.","explanation":"1990's → 1990s. Singular possessive ('s).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: 1990's → 1990s. ملكية المفرد."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write correct possessives.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence about one scientist's discovery.","options":[],"answer":"The scientist's discovery changed the way we treat diabetes.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses possessive apostrophes: singular ('s), plural (s'), irregular plural (children's), names ending in -s (James's); joint vs separate possession; its vs it's, their / there / they're, whose / who's; plural vs possessive (scientists / scientist's / scientists'); no apostrophe for plurals or decades (the 1990s) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence about several students' projects.","options":[],"answer":"The students' projects were displayed in the main hall.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses possessive apostrophes: singular ('s), plural (s'), irregular plural (children's), names ending in -s (James's); joint vs separate possession; its vs it's, their / there / they're, whose / who's; plural vs possessive (scientists / scientist's / scientists'); no apostrophe for plurals or decades (the 1990s) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write two sentences: one with \"its\" and one with \"it's\".","options":[],"answer":"The lab updated its equipment. It's now one of the best labs in the region.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses possessive apostrophes: singular ('s), plural (s'), irregular plural (children's), names ending in -s (James's); joint vs separate possession; its vs it's, their / there / they're, whose / who's; plural vs possessive (scientists / scientist's / scientists'); no apostrophe for plurals or decades (the 1990s) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Builder","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["The teacher reads a word and a short clue.","Teams build the word with letter tiles in 30 seconds.","The team holds up the word; correct spelling = 1 point, with correct capital letter or punctuation where needed = 1 bonus point.","Continue until all words are used."],"data":[{"word":"technique","clue":"a particular way of doing something that needs skill"},{"word":"precision","clue":"the quality of being very exact and accurate"},{"word":"potential","clue":"the possibility of developing or achieving something in the future"},{"word":"ethical","clue":"connected with what is morally right or wrong"},{"word":"controversial","clue":"causing a lot of disagreement"},{"word":"unprecedented","clue":"never having happened before"},{"word":"inherited","clue":"passed from parents to children through genes"},{"word":"long-term","clue":"lasting for a long time into the future"},{"word":"regulate","clue":"to control something with rules"},{"word":"implication","clue":"a possible effect or result of an action or decision"},{"word":"plausible","clue":"seeming reasonable and possibly true"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The ___ conclusions were controversial.","options":["committees's","committees","committee's","committee"],"answer":"committee's","explanation":"Singular possessive ('s).","explanation_ar":"ملكية المفرد."},{"q":"All the ___ results were checked twice.","options":["patients's","patients","patient's","patients'"],"answer":"patients'","explanation":"Plural possessive (s').","explanation_ar":"ملكية الجمع."},{"q":"The new rules protect ___ rights.","options":["people'","peoples'","peoples","people's"],"answer":"people's","explanation":"Irregular plural possessive (children's).","explanation_ar":"الجمع الشاذ."},{"q":"The researchers presented ___ findings at the conference.","options":["their","they're","their's","there"],"answer":"their","explanation":"its / it's, their / they're, whose / who's.","explanation_ar":"its أم it's."},{"q":"___ responsible for checking the data?","options":["Who's","Who'se","Whose","Whos"],"answer":"Who's","explanation":"its / it's, their / they're, whose / who's.","explanation_ar":"its أم it's."},{"q":"","options":["Adams and Faisal's experiments produced different results.","Adam's and Faisal's experiments produced different results.","Adam and Faisal's experiments produced different results each.","Adam's and Faisals experiments produced different results."],"answer":"Adam's and Faisal's experiments produced different results.","explanation":"Joint or separate possession.","explanation_ar":"الملكية المشتركة أو المنفصلة."},{"q":"","options":["In the 1960s, scientist's first discovered the structure of many proteins.","In the 1960s, scientists first discovered the structure of many proteins.","In the 1960's, scientists first discovered the structure of many proteins.","In the 1960s', scientists first discovered the structure of many proteins."],"answer":"In the 1960s, scientists first discovered the structure of many proteins.","explanation":"Plural or possessive? (no apostrophe for plurals).","explanation_ar":"جمع أم ملكية؟"},{"q":"The two ______ different interpretations of the data led to a heated debate at the conference.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["scientists's","scientists","scientist's","scientists'"],"answer":"scientists'","explanation":"Plural possessive (s').","explanation_ar":"ملكية الجمع."},{"q":"Coral reefs protect coastlines from storms, but ______ survival is threatened by rising ocean temperatures.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["there","their","its","they're"],"answer":"their","explanation":"its / it's, their / they're, whose / who's.","explanation_ar":"its أم it's."},{"q":"The museum's new exhibit explains how ______ immune systems differ from those of adults.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["childrens","children","children's","childrens'"],"answer":"children's","explanation":"Irregular plural possessive (children's).","explanation_ar":"الجمع الشاذ."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Science and Society” that shows possessive apostrophes: singular ('s), plural (s'), irregular plural (children's), names ending in -s (James's); joint vs separate possession; its vs it's, their / there / they're, whose / who's; plural vs possessive (scientists / scientist's / scientists'); no apostrophe for plurals or decades (the 1990s) in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Science and Society” that shows possessive apostrophes: singular ('s), plural (s'), irregular plural (children's), names ending in -s (James's); joint vs separate possession; its vs it's, their / there / they're, whose / who's; plural vs possessive (scientists / scientist's / scientists'); no apostrophe for plurals or decades (the 1990s) in action"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Builder”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Builder"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “The Scientists' Results, Its Effects: Possessives and Apostrophes (SAT)”. Let’s learn possessive apostrophes: singular ('s), plural (s'), irregular plural (children's), names ending in -s (James's); joint vs separate possession; its vs it's, their / there / they're, whose / who's; plural vs possessive (scientists / scientist's / scientists'); no apostrophe for plurals or decades (the 1990s) together. Singular noun → add 's: the scientist's notebook, Lina's project, James's lab (or James' – both are used; be consistent). Plural noun ending in -s → add only ': the scientists' results (more than one scientist). Irregular plural (not ending in -s) → add 's: the children's hospital, the people's opinions, women's health. Singular noun → add 's: the scientist's notebook, Lina's project, James's lab (or James' – both are used; be consistent). Plural noun ending in -s → add only ': the scientists' results (more than one scientist). Irregular plural (not ending in -s) → add 's: the children's hospital, the people's opinions, women's health. Joint possession (one thing shared) → apostrophe on the last name: Saad and Lina's project (one project). Separate possession → apostrophe on each name: Saad's and Lina's projects (two projects). Don't use apostrophes for ordinary plurals ((not: the scientist's agreed) → the scientists agreed) or decades (the 1990s). Here are some examples. The scientist's notebook was found in the lab. (one scientist) The scientists' results were published in a journal. (several scientists) The children's ward has new equipment. Be careful! Some learners say: “The scientist's agreed on the results.” That’s not right. We say: “The scientists agreed on the results.” Simple plural → no apostrophe. Quick check: \"The scientists' results\" – one scientist or more? … Think! … More than one. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"the scientists' results\" and \"the children's hospital\".","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS5","title":"Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat?: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to discuss a scientific controversy with appropriate caution.","Students will be able to write a balanced paragraph with hedging, precise vocabulary and correct possessives, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can discuss a scientific controversy in a balanced way.","I can write a careful, balanced paragraph."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من مناقشة قضية علمية مثيرة للجدل بحذر مناسب.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة فقرة متوازنة بلغة التحفّظ والمفردات الدقيقة والملكية الصحيحة، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أناقش قضية علمية مثيرة للجدل بطريقة متوازنة.","أستطيع أن أكتب فقرة حذرة ومتوازنة."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"technique","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tɛkˈnik/","definition":"a particular way of doing something that needs skill","arabic":"تقنية / أسلوب","example":"The new technique allows doctors to repair damaged cells.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"precision","pos":"noun","ipa":"/priˈsɪʒən/","definition":"the quality of being very exact and accurate","arabic":"دقة","example":"The robot operates with great precision.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"potential","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈtɛnʃəl/","definition":"the possibility of developing or achieving something in the future","arabic":"إمكانات / قدرة كامنة","example":"The new drug has enormous potential.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"inherited","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈhɛrətɪd/","definition":"passed from parents to children through genes","arabic":"وراثي / موروث","example":"Sickle cell disease is an inherited blood disorder.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"ethical","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɛθɪkəl/","definition":"connected with what is morally right or wrong","arabic":"أخلاقي","example":"Gene editing raises serious ethical questions.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"controversial","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌkɑntrəˈvɝʃəl/","definition":"causing a lot of disagreement","arabic":"مثير للجدل","example":"The experiment was highly controversial.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"long-term","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/lɔŋtɝm/","definition":"lasting for a long time into the future","arabic":"طويل الأمد","example":"Scientists don't yet know the long-term effects.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"regulate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈrɛɡjəˌleɪt/","definition":"to control something with rules","arabic":"ينظّم (بقوانين)","example":"Governments regulate how new medicines are tested.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"implication","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪmpləˈkeɪʃən/","definition":"a possible effect or result of an action or decision","arabic":"تبعة / انعكاس","example":"The discovery has important implications for medicine.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"plausible","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈplɔzəbəl/","definition":"seeming reasonable and possibly true","arabic":"معقول / مقنع","example":"The scientist offered a plausible explanation.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"conclusive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/kənˈklusɪv/","definition":"proving something completely","arabic":"قاطع / حاسم","example":"There is no conclusive evidence yet.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"tentative","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈtɛntətɪv/","definition":"not certain or final","arabic":"مبدئي / غير نهائي","example":"The results are tentative and need more testing.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"speculate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈspɛkjəˌleɪt/","definition":"to guess about something without enough information","arabic":"يخمّن / يتكهّن","example":"Journalists speculated about the cause of the illness.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"consent","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈsɛnt/","definition":"permission to do something","arabic":"موافقة","example":"Patients must give their consent before an operation.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"unprecedented","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ənˈprɛsɪˌdɛntɪd/","definition":"never having happened before","arabic":"غير مسبوق","example":"The pandemic caused unprecedented changes in daily life.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 2: hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, and possessives.\nA balanced paragraph presents both sides, uses hedging to avoid overstated claims, distinguishes tentative from conclusive evidence, and reaches a careful conclusion. Transitions such as however, on the other hand, nevertheless and therefore show how the arguments connect.\n✔ may well • is likely to • appears to • tends to • It seems that\n✔ may have been • must have • can't have\n✔ tentative, plausible, conclusive, ethical, implications\n✔ scientists' opinions • society's decisions • its (not it's)","examples":["The technology may well reduce greenhouse gases.","Early studies appear to show promising results.","The first tests may have been affected by small sample sizes.","Farmers' incomes could be affected.","The public's trust is essential.","The evidence is still inconclusive.","On the other hand, critics raise ethical concerns.","It seems that more research is needed."],"common_errors":["✗ Lab-grown meat will solve world hunger. → ✓ Lab-grown meat may help to reduce world hunger. (Hedge strong claims.)","✗ The scientist's opinions remain divided. (many scientists) → ✓ The scientists' opinions remain divided. (Plural possessive.)","✗ The first products may have be expensive. → ✓ The first products may have been expensive. (have + been.)"],"arabic":"نراجع لغة التحفّظ والتخمين والأفعال الناقصة التامة ومفردات النقاش العلمي والملكية.\nالفقرة المتوازنة تعرض الجانبين، وتستخدم التحفّظ لتجنّب المبالغة، وتميّز بين الأدلة المبدئية والقاطعة، وتصل إلى خلاصة حذرة.\nالروابط مثل however وon the other hand وnevertheless وtherefore توضّح العلاقة بين الحجج."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat?: Language in Use”. Let’s learn hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U2-LS5 | Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat?: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Science café: groups discuss a controversial technology (lab-grown meat, AI doctors, gene editing, cloud seeding for rain). Each student contributes one hedged prediction, one modal perfect about the past and one ethical question. The group writes a balanced conclusion.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 2: hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, and possessives. A balanced paragraph presents both sides, uses hedging to avoid overstated claims, distinguishes tentative from conclusive evidence, and reaches a careful conclusion. Transitions such as however, on the other hand, nevertheless and therefore show how the arguments connect.","on_screen":"✔ may well • is likely to • appears to • tends to • It seems that • ✔ may have been • must have • can't have • ✔ tentative, plausible, conclusive, ethical, implications","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The technology may well reduce greenhouse gases. Early studies appear to show promising results. The first tests may have been affected by small sample sizes.","on_screen":"The technology may well reduce greenhouse gases. / Early studies appear to show promising results. / The first tests may have been affected by small sample sizes.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Lab-grown meat will solve world hunger.” That’s not right. We say: “Lab-grown meat may help to reduce world hunger.” Hedge strong claims.","on_screen":"✗ Lab-grown meat will solve world hunger.   ✓ Lab-grown meat may help to reduce world hunger.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “The scientist's opinions remain divided. (many scientists)”? … Think! … It should be “The scientists' opinions remain divided.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can discuss a scientific controversy in a balanced way.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can discuss a scientific controversy in a balanced way.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع لغة التحفّظ والتخمين والأفعال الناقصة التامة ومفردات النقاش العلمي والملكية.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Lab-grown meat will solve world hunger. — الصحيح: Lab-grown meat may help to reduce world hunger.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أناقش قضية علمية مثيرة للجدل بطريقة متوازنة."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat? – Hamad","body":"Lab-grown meat, which is produced from animal cells in a laboratory, may well change the way we eat.\nSupporters argue that it is likely to reduce the land and water used for farming.\nHowever, the evidence so far appears to be tentative, and scientists' opinions remain divided.\nThe first products may have been too expensive for most people, but prices are expected to fall.\nSome consumers' concerns are ethical rather than scientific; they question whether such food is natural.\nIt seems reasonable to conclude that more research is needed before the technology is widely accepted.\nWhatever happens, society's decisions should be based on conclusive data, not on fear or hype.","word_count":110,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Hamad's paragraph. True or False?","items":[{"q":"Lab-grown meat is produced from animal cells in a laboratory.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat? – Hamad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Supporters argue that it will use more land and water.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat? – Hamad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Hamad says the evidence is conclusive.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat? – Hamad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Prices of lab-grown meat are expected to fall.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat? – Hamad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Some consumers' concerns are ethical.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat? – Hamad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Hamad thinks decisions should be based on fear.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat? – Hamad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Lab-grown meat ___ change the way we eat.","options":["may well to","well may to","may be well","may well"],"answer":"may well","explanation":"Hedging or speculation.","explanation_ar":"التحفّظ والتخمين."},{"q":"The evidence so far appears ___ tentative.","options":["be","to be","being","that is"],"answer":"to be","explanation":"Hedging or speculation.","explanation_ar":"التحفّظ والتخمين."},{"q":"The first products ___ too expensive for most people.","options":["may been","may have be","may have been","may had been"],"answer":"may have been","explanation":"Hedging or speculation.","explanation_ar":"التحفّظ والتخمين."},{"q":"___ opinions remain divided.","options":["Scientists'","Scientist's","Scientists's","Scientists"],"answer":"Scientists'","explanation":"Possessive or apostrophe.","explanation_ar":"الملكية."},{"q":"More research is needed because the evidence is still ___.","options":["conclusive","inherited","unprecedented","inconclusive"],"answer":"inconclusive","explanation":"Scientific debate vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات النقاش العلمي."},{"q":"Supporters say that lab-grown meat could reduce the land used for farming. ______ critics argue that the energy needed to produce it is still very high.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For example,","On the other hand,","Similarly,","Therefore,"],"answer":"On the other hand,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Science café: write your notes.","items":[{"q":"Your technology: ___","options":[],"answer":"Cloud seeding to increase rainfall","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A hedged prediction: ___","options":[],"answer":"Cloud seeding may well increase rainfall in dry regions.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A modal perfect about the past: ___","options":[],"answer":"The heavy rain last winter might have been caused by cloud seeding.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"An ethical question: ___","options":[],"answer":"Is it fair if one region's rain comes at another region's expense?","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A balanced conclusion: ___","options":[],"answer":"The technology appears promising; however, its long-term effects are not yet clear.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your balanced paragraph.","items":[{"q":"Plan: technology, benefits, risks, evidence, ethics, conclusion.","options":[],"answer":"AI doctors; faster diagnosis, available everywhere; mistakes, privacy; studies are tentative; patients' consent and trust; useful as a tool, not a replacement","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your balanced paragraph in 8–9 sentences.","options":[],"answer":"Artificial intelligence may well transform the way doctors diagnose diseases. AI systems are likely to analyze scans faster than humans, and they could make expert advice available in remote areas. Early studies appear to show impressive accuracy. However, the evidence is still tentative, and some systems' mistakes might have harmed patients if doctors had not checked them. There are also ethical questions about patients' privacy and consent. It seems that AI tends to perform best when it works alongside human doctors. Therefore, it is plausible that AI will become a valuable tool rather than a replacement. Society's trust will depend on careful regulation.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: hedges not overused? modal perfects correct? apostrophes in plural possessives? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"some system's mistakes\" to \"some systems' mistakes\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Quiz Show Challenge","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["Teams take turns choosing a category and a cue word.","Grammar: make a sentence with hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review). Words: give the meaning. Spelling: spell it. Write It!: write a sentence with correct punctuation.","Correct answers score 10 points; teams may steal a question another team misses.","The highest score after 8 minutes wins."],"data":[{"cue":"technique","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"precision","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"potential","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"inherited","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"ethical","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"controversial","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"long-term","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"regulate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"implication","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"plausible","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"conclusive","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"tentative","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"speculate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"consent","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"unprecedented","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The new battery is likely ___ the cost of electric cars.","options":["reducing","to reducing","to reduce","reduce"],"answer":"to reduce","explanation":"Hedging or speculation.","explanation_ar":"التحفّظ والتخمين."},{"q":"The fire ___ by a faulty wire, but investigators are still checking.","options":["may have been caused","may caused","may be caused yesterday","may have caused"],"answer":"may have been caused","explanation":"Hedging or speculation.","explanation_ar":"التحفّظ والتخمين."},{"q":"The results of the small study are ___ and need to be confirmed.","options":["unprecedented","inherited","conclusive","tentative"],"answer":"tentative","explanation":"Scientific debate vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات النقاش العلمي."},{"q":"The ___ new policy affects all of its laboratories.","options":["university","university's","universities","universitys'"],"answer":"university's","explanation":"Possessive or apostrophe.","explanation_ar":"الملكية."},{"q":"","options":["Its clear that the technology has enormous potential.","It's clear that the technology has enormous potential.","Its' clear that the technology has enormous potential.","It's' clear that the technology has enormous potential."],"answer":"It's clear that the technology has enormous potential.","explanation":"Possessive or apostrophe.","explanation_ar":"الملكية."},{"q":"Early tests of the new drug were very promising. ______ larger trials revealed side effects that had not appeared before.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Likewise,","For instance,","However,","Therefore,"],"answer":"However,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"Gene therapies can treat diseases at their source. ______ they may reduce the need for lifelong medication.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","In contrast,","As a result,","Nevertheless,"],"answer":"As a result,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• CRISPR is a gene-editing tool developed in 2012.\n• Its inventors won the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2020.\n• In 2023, the first CRISPR-based treatment was approved for sickle cell disease.\n• The treatment is very expensive.\n\nThe student wants to highlight both the promise and a limitation of CRISPR treatments. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Sickle cell disease is a blood disorder.","Although the first CRISPR-based treatment, approved in 2023, offers hope to patients with sickle cell disease, it is very expensive.","CRISPR was developed in 2012.","CRISPR's inventors won the Nobel Prize in 2020."],"answer":"Although the first CRISPR-based treatment, approved in 2023, offers hope to patients with sickle cell disease, it is very expensive.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Cloud seeding adds particles to clouds to encourage rain.\n• Saudi Arabia has run cloud-seeding programs in several regions.\n• Scientists are still studying how effective it is.\n• Results appear to vary depending on weather conditions.\n\nThe student wants to describe cloud seeding cautiously. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Saudi Arabia has several regions.","Clouds are made of water.","Cloud seeding definitely creates rain in all conditions.","Cloud seeding may encourage rain, but its effectiveness appears to vary with weather conditions and is still being studied."],"answer":"Cloud seeding may encourage rain, but its effectiveness appears to vary with weather conditions and is still being studied.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best sentence for a balanced scientific paragraph:","options":["The early results appear promising; however, the researchers' sample was small.","The early results prove everything; however, the researcher's sample was small.","The early results may possibly perhaps seem promising.","The early results appear promising, however the researchers sample was small."],"answer":"The early results appear promising; however, the researchers' sample was small.","explanation":"Hedging or speculation.","explanation_ar":"التحفّظ والتخمين."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A science café discussion: a plate with a lab-grown burger on one side and a traditional meal on the other; students at a round table holding cards \"Benefits\", \"Risks\", \"Evidence\", \"Ethics\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A science café discussion: a plate with a lab-grown burger on one side and a traditional meal on the other; students at a round table holding cards \"Benefits\", \"Risks\", \"Evidence\", \"Ethics\""},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Science and Society”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Science and Society”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L10-U2-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Quiz Show Challenge”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Quiz Show Challenge"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS5-T1","script":"Lab-grown meat, which is produced from animal cells in a laboratory, may well change the way we eat. Supporters argue that it is likely to reduce the land and water used for farming. However, the evidence so far appears to be tentative, and scientists' opinions remain divided. The first products may have been too expensive for most people, but prices are expected to fall. Some consumers' concerns are ethical rather than scientific; they question whether such food is natural. It seems reasonable to conclude that more research is needed before the technology is widely accepted. Whatever happens, society's decisions should be based on conclusive data, not on fear or hype.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U2-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Should We Trust Lab-Grown Meat?: Language in Use”. Let’s learn hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, possessives and apostrophes; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Science café: groups discuss a controversial technology (lab-grown meat, AI doctors, gene editing, cloud seeding for rain). Each student contributes one hedged prediction, one modal perfect about the past and one ethical question. The group writes a balanced conclusion. Today you use all of Unit 2: hedging and speculation, modal perfects, scientific debate vocabulary, and possessives. A balanced paragraph presents both sides, uses hedging to avoid overstated claims, distinguishes tentative from conclusive evidence, and reaches a careful conclusion. Transitions such as however, on the other hand, nevertheless and therefore show how the arguments connect. Here are some examples. The technology may well reduce greenhouse gases. Early studies appear to show promising results. The first tests may have been affected by small sample sizes. Be careful! Some learners say: “Lab-grown meat will solve world hunger.” That’s not right. We say: “Lab-grown meat may help to reduce world hunger.” Hedge strong claims. Quick check: what is wrong with “The scientist's opinions remain divided. (many scientists)”? … Think! … It should be “The scientists' opinions remain divided.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can discuss a scientific controversy in a balanced way.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"The discovery ___ lead to new treatments for heart disease.","options":["may well","may be well","well may to","may well to"],"answer":"may well","explanation":"Hedging modal (may, might, could, may well).","explanation_ar":"الفعل الناقص للتحفّظ."},{"q":"The new treatment is likely ___ next year.","options":["to be approved","being approved","to approve by","approve"],"answer":"to be approved","explanation":"be likely / unlikely / bound to.","explanation_ar":"be likely to."},{"q":"Teenagers ___ to sleep later than adults.","options":["are tending that","tend that","tends","tend"],"answer":"tend","explanation":"tend to for general patterns.","explanation_ar":"tend to."},{"q":"The results appear ___ the original hypothesis.","options":["supporting","that support","support","to support"],"answer":"to support","explanation":"appear / seem + to; It seems / appears that.","explanation_ar":"appear / seem."},{"q":"Which sentence is the most appropriately hedged for a scientific report?","options":["The data prove the treatment cures everyone.","The data suggest that the treatment may reduce symptoms.","The treatment is perfect.","Everyone agrees the treatment works."],"answer":"The data suggest that the treatment may reduce symptoms.","explanation":"Choose the right degree of certainty.","explanation_ar":"درجة اليقين."},{"q":"Although the new material performed well in laboratory tests, engineers warn that it ______ under the extreme heat of desert conditions.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["has never been tested anywhere","will certainly perform perfectly","always performs best","may not perform as well"],"answer":"may not perform as well","explanation":"Choose the right degree of certainty.","explanation_ar":"درجة اليقين."},{"q":"The ancient engineers ___ a great deal about water flow.","options":["must have known","must know","must knew","must have know"],"answer":"must have known","explanation":"must have + past participle (almost certain).","explanation_ar":"must have."},{"q":"She ___ the report; it was only published this morning.","options":["must have read","mustn't have read","can't have read","can't read yesterday"],"answer":"can't have read","explanation":"can't / couldn't have (almost certainly not).","explanation_ar":"can't have."},{"q":"The samples ___ during transport.","options":["may had contaminated","may have been contaminated","may have contaminated","may be contaminated yesterday"],"answer":"may have been contaminated","explanation":"Passive: modal + have been + past participle.","explanation_ar":"المجهول."},{"q":"The team ___ for hours when the power failed.","options":["must have been work","must have worked for hours","must have been working","must be working"],"answer":"must have been working","explanation":"Continuous: modal + have been + -ing.","explanation_ar":"المستمر."},{"q":"The evidence is ___; we need much more data before we can be sure.","options":["inconclusive","conclusive","unprecedented","inherited"],"answer":"inconclusive","explanation":"Scientific debate word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The writer never says it directly, but she ___ that the company was careless.","options":["implies","infers","effects","affects"],"answer":"implies","explanation":"Confusable pair (imply/infer, affect/effect, precise/accurate).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة."},{"q":"These rare ___ have puzzled scientists for decades.","options":["phenomenon","phenomena","phenomenas","phenomenons"],"answer":"phenomena","explanation":"Irregular academic plural.","explanation_ar":"الجمع الشاذ."},{"q":"Because the scientists could not yet explain why the patients had recovered, they offered only ______ explanations and called for further research.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["final","definite","conclusive","tentative"],"answer":"tentative","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The new rule will ___ thousands of laboratories.","options":["effect","infer","imply","affect"],"answer":"affect","explanation":"Confusable pair (imply/infer, affect/effect, precise/accurate).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات المتشابهة."},{"q":"The ___ results were checked by an independent laboratory.","options":["researchers's","researchers","researchers'","researcher"],"answer":"researchers'","explanation":"Irregular academic plural.","explanation_ar":"الجمع الشاذ."},{"q":"The hospital opened a new ___ wing.","options":["children'","childrens","children's","childrens'"],"answer":"children's","explanation":"Irregular plural possessive (children's).","explanation_ar":"الجمع الشاذ."},{"q":"The university updated ___ research guidelines.","options":["it is","its'","it's","its"],"answer":"its","explanation":"its / it's, their / they're, whose / who's.","explanation_ar":"its أم it's."},{"q":"","options":["Saad and Hudas joint study won an award.","Saad and Huda's joint study won an award.","Saads and Huda's joint study won an award.","Saad's and Huda's joint study won an award."],"answer":"Saad and Huda's joint study won an award.","explanation":"Joint or separate possession.","explanation_ar":"الملكية المشتركة أو المنفصلة."},{"q":"In the ______, scientists began using computers to model the climate, a development that transformed weather forecasting.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["1960s","1960's'","1960s'","1960's"],"answer":"1960s","explanation":"Plural or possessive? (no apostrophe for plurals).","explanation_ar":"جمع أم ملكية؟"},{"q":"Many people assume that new medicines are tested quickly. ______ most new drugs take more than ten years to move from the laboratory to the pharmacy.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Therefore,","For this reason,","In reality,","Similarly,"],"answer":"In reality,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Antibiotics kill bacteria.\n• Overusing antibiotics can make bacteria resistant.\n• Resistant bacteria are harder to treat.\n• Doctors now prescribe antibiotics more carefully.\n\nThe student wants to explain why doctors prescribe antibiotics carefully. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Doctors prescribe many medicines.","Because overusing antibiotics can make bacteria resistant and harder to treat, doctors now prescribe them more carefully.","Antibiotics kill bacteria.","Bacteria can live in many places."],"answer":"Because overusing antibiotics can make bacteria resistant and harder to treat, doctors now prescribe them more carefully.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"the scientist's must of made a mistake with it's data.\"","options":["The scientists must of made a mistake with their data.","The scientists must have made a mistake with their data.","the scientists must have made a mistake with their data.","The scientist's must have made a mistake with its data."],"answer":"The scientists must have made a mistake with their data.","explanation":"must have + past participle (almost certain).","explanation_ar":"must have."},{"q":"\"The fossil is likely to have been buried in a flood.\" How certain is the writer?","options":["not interested in the evidence","sure it did not happen","fairly certain, but not completely sure","completely certain"],"answer":"fairly certain, but not completely sure","explanation":"be likely / unlikely / bound to.","explanation_ar":"be likely to."}],"theme_ar":"العلم والمجتمع","essential_question_ar":"مَن ينبغي أن يقرّر إلى أي حدّ يمكن أن يذهب العلم؟","can_do":"I can express degrees of certainty with academic hedging, speculate about the past with modal perfects, use precise scientific and ethical vocabulary, and use apostrophes for possession correctly.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أعبّر عن درجات اليقين بلغة التحفّظ الأكاديمية، وأخمّن عن الماضي بالأفعال الناقصة التامة، وأستخدم مفردات علمية وأخلاقية دقيقة، وأستخدم الفاصلة العليا للملكية بشكل صحيح."});
